<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful
    =============================

    Shared library: the publish and subscribe event broker. One of the six shared libraries under
    shared/ (Kernel, Diagnostics, Eventful, Localization, Containers, Contracts) that sit BENEATH the
    four Phase 1 services. AAP §0.1.5 requires that layer to carry "only pure behaviour and no I/O",
    and this project holds to it literally: no host, no socket, no endpoint, no serializer.

    Legacy sources ported into this folder, per AAP §0.3.1 and §0.4.2.3:

        EventBroker.cs          ws_objects/pfw.utility.invoker.pbl.src/n_cst_eventful.sru
                                1,328 lines of PowerScript. Preserves the subscription priorities,
                                the lexical ordering symbols, the persistence namespace and the
                                exception capture at :L865 to :L890 verbatim.
        SubscriptionTopic.cs    ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/se_cst_dw.sru:L47-L76
                                ws_objects/pfw.thread.pbl.src/n_cst_threading.sru:L544, :L596
                                Decomposes the fused legacy topic string into its three independent
                                encodings, sequence and name and lifetime, and reconstitutes the
                                fused form only at the compatibility edge.
        VetoResult.cs           the broker's veto protocol. Tri valued (PREVENT_ONCE is 1,
                                PREVENT_DEEP is 2, continue is the third state) and never flattened
                                to a boolean, because flattening silently converts a deep prevention
                                into a shallow one.
        SubscriptionOptions.cs  the priority, ordering symbol and namespace surface.

    WHY THIS IS A SHARED LIBRARY RATHER THAN A MEMBER OF ONE SERVICE
    Both in scope services consume the broker, verified two independent ways, so neither DataServices
    nor Persistence can own it:
        ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/se_cst_dw.sru:L6-L7 declares
            "type eventful from n_cst_eventful within se_cst_dw", and :L33 holds the instance member.
        ws_objects/pfw.thread.pbl.src/n_cst_threading_eventful.sru:L4 declares
            "global type n_cst_threading_eventful from n_cst_eventful".
    It holds shared IMPLEMENTATION, consumed in process through a ProjectReference. It is NEVER a
    cross service channel: AAP §0.7.3 C-A makes shared/PowerFramework.Contracts the only published
    cross service coupling in the refactor, and this project must not become a back door around it.
    Hence no gRPC package, no OpenAPI package, no ASP.NET Core framework reference, and no hosting or
    dependency injection package here, and none may be added.

    THE LEGACY TREE IS READ ONLY (AAP §0.7.3 C-C) AND SHARES THIS WORKING DIRECTORY
    The .sru paths above are the behavioural oracle: read as specification, never edited, moved or
    reformatted, and never a build input. Nothing under ws_objects/ and no *.pbl, *.pbt, *.pbw, *.pbr
    or *.pbd is compiled or embedded by this project, which is why there is no ItemGroup below naming
    a legacy path. The root .dockerignore also keeps ws_objects/ out of the build context, so such a
    read would fail in a container and in CI even if it happened to work locally.

    PROVENANCE: A CLEAN CREATION, NOT A TRANSLATED BUILD FILE
    The legacy library pfw.utility.invoker.pbl appears on the pfw target's ordered library list
    [pfw.pbt:L8], which is the only "build definition" the legacy has for this code. That ordering is
    semantically significant in PowerBuilder because it resolves one flat global namespace, and
    ProjectReference has no ordering analogue, so it is deliberately not encoded here. The two
    PowerBuilder project objects are not translated either: AAP §0.4.2.1 and §0.8.6 R5 record that
    they disagree on library count and on vendor and that both declare pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl,
    which does not exist anywhere in the repository, so neither would build as written. They are
    REFERENCE for build intent only. PowerBuilder has no namespaces and no import statements
    (AAP §0.4.5.1), so there is nothing to rewrite: this project file is what establishes
    PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful as that namespace's compilation unit.

    GOVERNING CONSTRAINTS. review_rules reports "No user rules provided", so no user specified rule
    governs this file and none was invented; that absence is not latitude. In their place AAP §0.7.2
    (the enterprise baseline) and §0.7.3 (the twelve binding non rule constraints) apply, and each
    decision below cites the constraint it discharges. Structural authorities: §0.3.1 names this file,
    §0.5.4.3 classifies every project file as a CREATE, §0.3.2.1 fixes the validated build mechanism,
    and §0.5.4.1 makes central package management mandatory rather than optional.

    DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED HERE, because every one is inherited by MSBuild directory walk up from
    the repository root Directory.Build.props, the single point of control for every project
    (there is no intervening Directory.Build.props under shared/):

        TargetFramework net10.0, LangVersion, Nullable enable, ImplicitUsings enable,
        TreatWarningsAsErrors true, EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel,
        ManagePackageVersionsCentrally true, Deterministic, ContinuousIntegrationBuild,
        Product, Company, Copyright.

    Those names appear immediately above as documentation and nowhere else: not one of them is
    declared in this file. Strip the comments and what remains is three properties and one item.

    Restating any of them would be a defect even where the value happened to match, because it
    silently decouples this project from that single point of control. AAP §0.7.3 C-I is the reason:
    each service must build and test independently from a clean checkout, so the documented per
    service command succeeds transitively wherever a service reaches this library, and a locally
    restated property is exactly the seam through which one project drifts from the other fifteen.
    Nullable reference types and warnings as errors therefore apply here in full: under the inherited
    gate a single compiler or analyzer warning fails this build, and no NoWarn blanket softens it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Both values equal the defaults MSBuild derives from this file name, and are stated anyway so
         that a future directory rename cannot silently change the namespace and assembly identity
         that the four .cs files here, the sibling test project and the consuming services all
         resolve against. The string is fixed by the AAP §0.4.5.1 namespace scheme
         PowerFramework.Shared.{Kernel, Diagnostics, Eventful, Localization, Containers} and must
         stay exactly this: shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful.Tests references this project by
         this exact path, and the produced assembly must be PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful.dll. -->
    <RootNamespace>PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful</AssemblyName>
    <Description>PowerFramework shared event broker: publish and subscribe dispatch with subscription priorities, lexically ordered delivery, a decomposed subscription topic carrying sequence and name and lifetime separately, and the tri valued veto protocol. Ported from the pfw.utility.invoker.pbl n_cst_eventful object.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Project references: exactly one, and it must stay that way">
    <!--
      Constraint discharged here: AAP §0.7.3 C-I, each service builds and tests independently.

      Kernel is the SOLE dependency of this library, and that is a measured finding rather than a
      convention. Counted over all 1,328 lines of n_cst_eventful.sru, the broker consumes:

          RetCode.OK, RetCode.FAILED, RetCode.E_INVALID_ARGUMENT, RetCode.E_INVALID_OBJECT and
          RetCode.E_EVENT_NOT_FOUND     24 use sites
          Predicates.IsValidObject       4 use sites
          Predicates.IsPrevented         2 use sites
          Predicates.IsFailed            1 use site
          ThrowException                 1 use site

      All five land in PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel. The tri state boundary those predicates carry
      (a prevention reads as a success; cancelled and null are neither) is behaviour preserved by
      REUSE, never reimplemented locally where it could drift from the algebra every other project
      shares. Predicates.IsPrevented matters twice over here, because the broker's veto is the
      producer of the PREVENT value that boundary is built around.

      It is DELIBERATELY NOT a reference to PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics, even though the
      broker's exception path handles an assertion failure. n_cst_eventful.sru:L873 tests
      ClassName(ex) = "assertionfailed", a STRING class name comparison, and :L876 then reads the
      detail members off it. EventBroker.cs reproduces that with a runtime type test plus a locally
      declared detail carrier abstraction precisely so this project takes no second reference:
      adding one would contradict the measured single dependency and couple two shared libraries for
      a coupling the legacy itself never expressed in its type system.

      C-I also bounds this list. Nothing outside shared/ may be referenced, and within shared/
      nothing but Kernel: no PowerFramework.Contracts (that is the published boundary, not shared
      implementation), no other PowerFramework.Shared.* project, and nothing under services/. Nor
      any of the four deferred services (DesignSystem, Documents, Integration, ScriptBridge), which
      AAP §0.2.2.2 forbids implementing even as a stub and which therefore have no project to name.
      Relative path only: never an absolute path, and never a package standing in for a project.

      Left deliberately public rather than hidden behind PrivateAssets. The sibling test project
      takes no direct Kernel reference of its own and reaches RetCode, Predicates and ThrowException
      transitively through this edge, so narrowing it would break that project.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ZERO PackageReference elements, and zero is the correct count (AAP §0.7.3 C-A, §0.1.5).

    This is a verified finding, not an oversight. The broker is the largest pure PowerScript object
    in the in scope set and it binds nothing native: searching n_cst_eventful.sru for a native class
    binding returns no match across all 1,328 lines, and its "type prototypes" block is empty, so it
    declares no classic external function either. AAP §0.6.5 states the same conclusion structurally,
    recording that the shared kernel, the DataWindow service layer and both threading libraries
    contain zero PBNI objects. With no native binding to replace there is no substitution to buy, and
    the port needs only the Base Class Library: collections for the subscription table, System.Type
    and System.String for the class name chain the legacy builds, System.Exception and its Data
    dictionary for the exception capture, and composite formatting for the message assembly.

    Nothing in this project performs I/O, networking, logging, serialization or database access, so no
    ASP.NET reference, no web SDK and no third party package belongs in it. Do not add one "to be
    safe": an unnecessary reference would drag a NuGet audit surface onto a library that both in scope
    services depend on. If a package ever does become genuinely necessary, add it VERSIONLESS and pin
    the version in the repository root Directory.Packages.props. ManagePackageVersionsCentrally is
    enabled repository wide (AAP §0.5.4.1), so an inline Version attribute here is a restore error
    (NU1008) rather than a shortcut, and a floating, wildcard or "latest" version is barred outright
    by the §0.7.2 baseline. Because this project declares no package, no NU1903 advisory can originate
    in it; one appearing at restore would have arrived transitively through Kernel and must be
    reported, never suppressed from here.
  -->

  <!--
    Also deliberately absent, each for a stated reason, so the omissions read as decisions:

      * OutputType. This is a class library and must stay one; the SDK default of Library is correct.
        Directory.Build.props:L203 to L206 reserves OutputType as a per project concern precisely
        because the tree needs three different answers, and the Exe value belongs ONLY to the hand
        authored xunit.v3 test projects, which fail to build without it. Setting it here would turn a
        library into an application and break every consumer.
      * EnableDefaultCompileItems, and any explicit Compile, Content, None, EmbeddedResource or
        Remove item. SDK style projects glob the four .cs files in this directory automatically and
        they all sit flat here, so there is nothing to add and nothing to exclude. An explicit list
        would be worse than redundant: it would silently drop any file added later.
      * InternalsVisibleTo. Every type and member of the broker is public, because the legacy object
        is a global class whose whole surface is called from other objects, so the sibling test
        project and the consuming services reach all of it without one.
      * NoWarn, WarningsNotAsErrors and any project wide analyzer suppression. The only suppressions
        this refactor needs are the naming ones for the preserved SCREAMING_SNAKE constant
        identifiers, and AAP §0.4.5.3 keeps those file glob scoped in the repository root
        .editorconfig. A suppression here would broaden that scope to this whole project.
      * GenerateDocumentationFile. Inherited TreatWarningsAsErrors turns a missing XML documentation
        comment (CS1591) into a build error, and no requirement asks for a documentation file.
      * PackageId, Version, IsPackable, GeneratePackageOnBuild and all other packaging metadata.
        Nothing in this refactor publishes these libraries as NuGet packages; they are consumed by
        ProjectReference, and the shipped artifacts are four container images, one per in scope
        service.
      * Any solution filter. .NET 10 emits the XML .slnx solution format, and no .slnf filter is
        authored because a filter's project list must be kept in exact agreement with the solution
        BY HAND and every drift fails hard. AAP §0.3.2.1's MSB4014-in-all-three-verbs claim was
        measured false on the pinned SDK; docs/BUILD.md section 2 carries the correction.
        Build scoping is done by the per service .slnx files together with the root property sheets.
  -->

</Project>
